<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1758">1758</a>–<a href="/years/1806">1806</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean-Jacques Dessalines was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>. After the defeat of the French expedition, he declared the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> in 1804 and became its first ruler. The loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> was a major blow to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines.jpg" alt="Jean-Jacques Dessalines" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis Rigaud · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Jacques_Dessalines.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Haitian Revolution of 1791 to 1804, which destroyed Saint-Domingue and produced the first Black republic in history under the leadership of Toussaint Louverture and <b>Jean-Jacques Dessalines</b>, was itself a direct consequence of the first empire's plantation economy and a transformative moment in Atlantic history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Haiti, the Taíno name for Hispaniola, was reclaimed in 1804 by <b>Jean-Jacques Dessalines</b> for the state the enslaved people of French Saint-Domingue had won.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti">Republic of Haiti · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic of Haiti arose from the crisis that followed the assassination of Emperor Jacques I, the former <b>Jean-Jacques Dessalines</b>, near Port-au-Prince on 17 October 1806.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Spain and Britain invaded; 7,000 French Republican troops landed in September 1792; Toussaint Louverture and <b>Jean-Jacques Dessalines</b>, leading the formerly leaderless revolt, first allied with Spain, then destroyed the Spanish forces and joined the Republic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>France conceded defeat in 1803, and <b>Jean-Jacques Dessalines</b> proclaimed the republic of Haiti.</blockquote><a href="/religion/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1802 Napoleon sent an army to retake the colony, and French forces restored slavery in Guadeloupe, but Saint-Domingue’s Black and mixed-race generals defeated the expedition, and <b>Jean-Jacques Dessalines</b> proclaimed the independence of Haiti on 1 January 1804.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Grande-Rivière-du-Nord, in what is now <a href="/countries/haiti">Haiti</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/haiti">Haiti</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.58,-72.18&amp;year=1789" aria-label="Grande-Rivière-du-Nord on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2601 1438 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jean-Jacques Dessalines"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3051 1708h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3051 1708h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.58,-72.18&amp;year=1789">What was here in 1789? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
